WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:03.220
- In my mind, I want to be able

00:00:03.220 --> 00:00:06.130
to say if you are here, I will serve you.

00:00:06.130 --> 00:00:08.930
Whatever you need, we will make it happen

00:00:08.930 --> 00:00:10.420
but that's not the truth.

00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:15.420
The truth is budgets are
fueled by enrollment.

00:00:15.810 --> 00:00:17.230
- [Narrator] In Michigan's capital city,

00:00:17.230 --> 00:00:19.190
the Lansing School District student count

00:00:19.190 --> 00:00:20.840
had been shrinking for years.

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:22.190
But prior to the pandemic,

00:00:22.190 --> 00:00:23.883
those losses were leveling out.

00:00:23.883 --> 00:00:26.440
Then last year, as students, parents

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:28.380
and their schools
struggled to stay connected

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:29.650
during the pandemic,

00:00:29.650 --> 00:00:32.510
the district experienced a
drop of almost 1,000 students

00:00:32.510 --> 00:00:36.460
from the year prior, a 9.2% decrease.

00:00:36.460 --> 00:00:41.460
- We count on the walls
of schools so heavily,

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:44.890
and the walls kind of
like keep us gathered

00:00:44.890 --> 00:00:46.950
in this one central location,

00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:50.910
and we did not have that over
the past year and a half.

00:00:50.910 --> 00:00:52.420
- [Narrator] That
worrisome trend played out

00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:55.210
in public school districts
nationwide last year.

00:00:55.210 --> 00:00:57.030
And this fall, it's unclear how many

00:00:57.030 --> 00:00:58.684
of those students will return.

00:00:58.684 --> 00:01:02.863
- This upcoming school
year is new for everyone.

00:01:02.863 --> 00:01:05.650
It's pivotal, there's
no question about that.

00:01:05.650 --> 00:01:07.700
- [Narrator] So what's at
stake for schools long term

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:09.700
if they can't recover student numbers lost

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:11.120
during the pandemic?

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:13.407
- Traditionally, public school enrollment

00:01:13.407 --> 00:01:15.320
and school funding are linked.

00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:18.450
As a school has more students enrolled,

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:20.920
they get more funding from the state.

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:22.383
- [Narrator] When it comes
to that state funding,

00:01:22.383 --> 00:01:24.810
enrollment in Michigan
is normally evaluated

00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:26.880
on two count days during the school year,

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:29.900
which means regular student
attendance is crucial.

00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:32.820
During the pandemic, many
states, including Michigan,

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:35.149
eased restrictions around
how students are counted.

00:01:35.149 --> 00:01:38.760
This helped to limit reductions
in per pupil state aid.

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:43.000
Another safety net, funding
from the COVID-19 Relief Law.

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:44.990
In 2020, the Lansing School District

00:01:44.990 --> 00:01:49.060
received $8,227 per student in state aid,

00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:51.860
which initially meant
approximately $84 million

00:01:51.860 --> 00:01:53.430
in state funding.

00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:55.450
Without those more flexible policies,

00:01:55.450 --> 00:01:58.220
the district says last
year's student count decline

00:01:58.220 --> 00:02:00.873
could have resulted in a
loss of around $6 million.

00:02:02.060 --> 00:02:03.530
This year, student counts

00:02:03.530 --> 00:02:06.810
are largely returning to
pre-pandemic policies.

00:02:06.810 --> 00:02:07.643
- If schools aren't able

00:02:07.643 --> 00:02:10.740
to bring enough students back to school,

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:14.510
they will be forced to deal
with some rather significant

00:02:14.510 --> 00:02:16.280
and awful decisions to make,

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:18.695
whether it's cuts to personnel,

00:02:18.695 --> 00:02:22.740
programming cuts, those
are all closely tied

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:25.460
to the amount of students
they have enrolled.

00:02:25.460 --> 00:02:27.260
- [Narrator] Enrollment
declines don't necessarily mean

00:02:27.260 --> 00:02:29.040
a school's costs go down.

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:30.160
So when budgets are cut,

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:32.370
it often means schools
are left with less money

00:02:32.370 --> 00:02:33.940
to pay for the staffing and programs

00:02:33.940 --> 00:02:36.260
that the school still needs.

00:02:36.260 --> 00:02:39.140
Nationally, grades that saw
the most significant drop-offs

00:02:39.140 --> 00:02:40.940
were kindergarten and pre-K,

00:02:40.940 --> 00:02:43.640
grades that are not
compulsory in many states.

00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:46.220
That enrollment trend
also occurred in Lansing.

00:02:46.220 --> 00:02:48.850
- Families just didn't
enroll their children

00:02:48.850 --> 00:02:50.630
and so they were just waiting out

00:02:50.630 --> 00:02:51.500
for another year.

00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:53.570
They basically red shirted their kids.

00:02:53.570 --> 00:02:55.550
- [Narrator] Experts say they
initially believed enrollment

00:02:55.550 --> 00:02:57.830
in younger students
would tick up this fall.

00:02:57.830 --> 00:02:59.040
And that kindergarten classes

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:00.950
might even be overcrowded.

00:03:00.950 --> 00:03:02.300
But the spread of the Delta variant

00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:04.461
is throwing that into question.

00:03:04.461 --> 00:03:07.850
(telephone beeping)

00:03:07.850 --> 00:03:09.350
- This is Jekeia Murphy.

00:03:09.350 --> 00:03:11.410
I'm the principal at Reo Elementary.

00:03:11.410 --> 00:03:12.300
How are you?

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:14.500
Do you know what the
first day of school is?

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:18.050
I am talking to teachers

00:03:18.050 --> 00:03:19.420
and I'm talking to parents.

00:03:19.420 --> 00:03:22.223
What do we do to keep
these kids in school?

00:03:23.650 --> 00:03:24.806
Simply put.

00:03:24.806 --> 00:03:25.800
- [Narrator] Of the students

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:27.500
who officially left Reo last year,

00:03:27.500 --> 00:03:30.740
Murphy says the reasons for
those departures varied.

00:03:30.740 --> 00:03:32.900
Some moved away, others shifted to schools

00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:34.650
offering in-person learning.

00:03:34.650 --> 00:03:39.350
- I lost parents because
they needed students

00:03:39.350 --> 00:03:40.280
in the classroom.

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:43.430
I lost students to charter schools

00:03:43.430 --> 00:03:46.130
within the Lansing
school district boundary.

00:03:46.130 --> 00:03:49.270
As a parent, I always
left those conversations

00:03:49.270 --> 00:03:50.773
saying I get it.

00:03:51.940 --> 00:03:53.510
I get it.

00:03:53.510 --> 00:03:55.270
- [Narrator] Murphy says
the larger issue at Reo,

00:03:55.270 --> 00:03:59.440
where 87% of kids are eligible
for free or reduced lunch,

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:01.020
was with families who disengaged

00:04:01.020 --> 00:04:02.880
or burned out from virtual learning.

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:05.020
- I would say our preschool kids,

00:04:05.020 --> 00:04:08.570
and our kindergarten
kids disengaged early on

00:04:08.570 --> 00:04:10.090
in the school year.

00:04:10.090 --> 00:04:12.160
And it was easier for those parents

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:14.530
because I asked, it was
easier for those parents

00:04:14.530 --> 00:04:17.323
to say we'll see you when
we can come in person.

00:04:18.340 --> 00:04:19.620
- [Narrator] This fall is the first time

00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:20.740
the Lansing School District

00:04:20.740 --> 00:04:23.360
is welcoming students back in person.

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:25.550
Shuldiner and his team put
together different budgets

00:04:25.550 --> 00:04:27.540
based on potential enrollment numbers

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:29.600
because the district's
budget won't be finalized

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:30.700
for this school year

00:04:30.700 --> 00:04:32.770
until after the fall student count,

00:04:32.770 --> 00:04:34.760
which takes place in October.

00:04:34.760 --> 00:04:36.410
- I've got some doomsday budgets

00:04:36.410 --> 00:04:38.830
about what it looks like at 8,000 kids,

00:04:38.830 --> 00:04:41.740
and that was about are you
gonna shut a school down?

00:04:41.740 --> 00:04:43.650
Are you going to close some programs?

00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:45.080
Are you gonna do certain things?

00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:48.790
And then I had some wonderful
rainbow in the sky kind

00:04:48.790 --> 00:04:50.430
of things that at 11,000,

00:04:50.430 --> 00:04:52.800
wow, we might be able
to reopen some programs

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:54.733
that we had shuttered 20 years ago.

00:04:56.240 --> 00:04:57.690
- [Narrator] So how are
public schools working

00:04:57.690 --> 00:04:59.095
to recover students?

00:04:59.095 --> 00:05:01.540
Shuldiner outlined three
enrollment initiatives

00:05:01.540 --> 00:05:03.180
that Lansing is prioritizing.

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:04.210
- [Ben] First and foremost

00:05:04.210 --> 00:05:06.460
is that we've announced universal pre-K.

00:05:06.460 --> 00:05:08.300
That for me is a linchpin

00:05:08.300 --> 00:05:10.967
to what a district should always be doing.

00:05:10.967 --> 00:05:12.400
- [Narrator] Shuldiner
hopes it improves enrollment

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:13.400
in the long term.

00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:14.530
- We looked at some numbers

00:05:14.530 --> 00:05:15.728
and we basically said wow,

00:05:15.728 --> 00:05:20.728
the students that start with
us in pre-K, stay with us.

00:05:20.860 --> 00:05:22.490
- [Narrator] Second,
the district created a K

00:05:22.490 --> 00:05:23.710
through 12 online school

00:05:23.710 --> 00:05:26.490
for anyone who prefers remote instruction.

00:05:26.490 --> 00:05:27.840
The online school will also allow

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:29.134
the district some flexibility

00:05:29.134 --> 00:05:31.770
so that kids enrolled
in in-person instruction

00:05:31.770 --> 00:05:34.200
who need to quarantine because of COVID-19

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:35.770
can continue to attend school

00:05:35.770 --> 00:05:37.078
and still be counted.

00:05:37.078 --> 00:05:39.270
The third enrollment initiative focuses

00:05:39.270 --> 00:05:40.733
on outreach to the community.

00:05:41.650 --> 00:05:44.046
- Instead of them coming
to us, we're pushing out

00:05:44.046 --> 00:05:47.450
because we're trying to
get enrollment numbers up,

00:05:47.450 --> 00:05:50.710
and create new relationships.

00:05:50.710 --> 00:05:51.543
- [Announcer] We've got enrollment

00:05:51.543 --> 00:05:53.540
from anybody from K to 12,

00:05:53.540 --> 00:05:55.246
for any of the Lansing public schools,

00:05:55.246 --> 00:05:57.060
so make sure you see that booth.

00:05:57.060 --> 00:06:00.750
- I will never be unprepared
to enroll a student.

00:06:00.750 --> 00:06:02.480
You don't have to come to me.

00:06:02.480 --> 00:06:03.623
I will find you.

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:06.560
So if I'm at the grocery store

00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:07.621
and I see a little kid,

00:06:07.621 --> 00:06:08.850
I'm like hey, how old are you?

00:06:08.850 --> 00:06:10.820
What school do you go to?

00:06:10.820 --> 00:06:12.160
- [Narrator] Murphy says she and her team

00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:13.410
are working to solve problems

00:06:13.410 --> 00:06:14.930
for families and answer questions

00:06:14.930 --> 00:06:16.250
that might be preventing parents

00:06:16.250 --> 00:06:18.260
from bringing students back to school.

00:06:18.260 --> 00:06:19.720
- Do you need transportation.

00:06:19.720 --> 00:06:21.230
- No.

00:06:21.230 --> 00:06:23.850
- Do you need before or after care?

00:06:23.850 --> 00:06:25.040
- I don't think so.

00:06:25.040 --> 00:06:26.462
- Okay, I think you're all good.

00:06:26.462 --> 00:06:27.295
- All right.

00:06:27.295 --> 00:06:28.656
- [Narrator] Once the
academic year begins,

00:06:28.656 --> 00:06:30.910
school leaders like Murphy need to ensure

00:06:30.910 --> 00:06:33.870
that those students who
did enroll keep showing up.

00:06:33.870 --> 00:06:36.310
- [Bryan] Schools need to
be really hyper-focused

00:06:36.310 --> 00:06:39.240
on the supports that
they provide to educators

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:41.810
as those educators then provide supports

00:06:41.810 --> 00:06:44.630
to students as they return
back to the classrooms.

00:06:44.630 --> 00:06:46.650
- Plop it right in that hole.

00:06:46.650 --> 00:06:48.900
- [Narrator] That return
looks increasingly complicated

00:06:48.900 --> 00:06:50.930
as districts around the
country face challenges

00:06:50.930 --> 00:06:53.070
like staffing shortages
and quarantines due

00:06:53.070 --> 00:06:55.240
to the spread of COVID-19.

00:06:55.240 --> 00:06:57.890
Lansing School District has
implemented a mask mandate

00:06:57.890 --> 00:07:00.130
for anyone entering district buildings,

00:07:00.130 --> 00:07:03.120
and a vaccine requirement for staff.

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:05.780
Still, Shuldiner says
without renewed policies

00:07:05.780 --> 00:07:07.316
that allow for more flexibility,

00:07:07.316 --> 00:07:09.150
attendance and student counts

00:07:09.150 --> 00:07:10.720
may be threatened by quarantines

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:13.440
or school closures due to COVID-19.

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:16.130
- So if that means I need
to have conversations

00:07:16.130 --> 00:07:19.710
with parents about why
their enrollment matters

00:07:19.710 --> 00:07:21.570
to us collectively at Reo,

00:07:21.570 --> 00:07:23.500
I need to get my talking points together

00:07:23.500 --> 00:07:26.090
and make sure that that is communicated

00:07:26.090 --> 00:07:28.370
in a kind, sensitive way

00:07:28.370 --> 00:07:29.840
but one that is effective

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:31.913
and resonates with all of us

00:07:31.913 --> 00:07:33.950
because we do need all of us

00:07:33.950 --> 00:07:35.206
in order for this to work.

00:07:35.206 --> 00:07:37.873
(pensive music)

